1661005330345111841762,400.27346013761179050841625592987,0.4568183128311407129283440041123,227.12591091348919103204257452921,199.00665213776794207936587888116,174.96321843860518235634914400648\\r\\nJoint margin=.08 WACC=.14,sensitivity,p_24ab0dd948042645,bull,bounded_optional,True,True,True,null,0.81360215004658730591580233371095,11.646780705526622869332398741372,0,0,653.2775,94.815280705526622869332398741373,154.57829008462116218032799287195,678.63639462268095270282364234169,332.38775782209115103030350505166,0.4036635971077768207379095661012,188.60574030034447240060803269983,165.25546028710584306911101788163,145.28975230621373060502150498125\\r\\nJoint margin=.08 WACC=.16,sensitivity,p_d1f06b20ba76032b,bull,bounded_optional,True,True,True,null,0.81360215004658730591580233371095,11.646780705526622869332398741372,0,0,653.2775,94.815280705526622869332398741373,154.57829008462116218032799287195,574.23079544996080613315846659682,285.38996391134609293007160059752,0.3615129369258030833498895860803,161.93792987585966719423027297180,141.88925054433977452354996026658,124.74658344532734093102544719455\\r\\nPerpetually growing terminal reserve,sensitivity,p_f5e00068d9ab8d4b,bull,bounded_optional,True,True,True,null,0.81360215004658730591580233371095,11.646780705526622869332398741372,0,0,653.2775,94.815280705526622869332398741373,154.57829008462116218032799287195,678.63639462268095270282364234169,332.38775782209115103030350505166,0.4036635971077768207379095661012,188.60574030034447240060803269983,165.25546028710584306911101788163,145.28975230621373060502150498125\\r\\n\"","\"# Rajputana Industries Limited: capacity growth does not yet establish cash-funded compounding\\n\\n## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\\n**Funding-and-execution hold, not a compounder.** This is the non-ferrous scrap-to-metal-products manufacturer Rajputana Industries Limited, NSE **RAJINDLTD**, ISIN **INE0PCU01012**, not Rajputana Biodiesel, Rajputana Stainless or Rajputana Investment & Finance. Physical output and operating profit have grown, and expanded capacity creates an identifiable opportunity. The decisive weakness is the capital and cash required to sustain that growth: FY26 reported operating cash flow includes a large increase in supplier financing. After reclassification, cash conversion is deeply negative, while old supplier-finance maturities expired before the entry date and current committed liquidity is unverified. At ₹61, a low-looking earnings multiple is not sufficient. This is a cyclical conversion/capacity thesis with refinancing dependence, not a proven high-return organic compounder. Auditable entry liquidity, supplier-finance continuity, related LLP terms and external-volume economics could change the conclusion; a lower price alone cannot fix a funding gap. [FY26 annual report](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/RIL%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf)\\n\\n## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\\n- Research information cutoff: **20 October 2026, Asia/Kolkata**. Valuation quote: official NSE close **19 October 2026**, not an intraday or subsequent price. Later retrieval does not make later information eligible.\\n- Scope: standalone Ind AS operating company. Shera group rating aggregates are not issuer accounts. Five audited financial years FY22–FY26, with FY21 nominal revenue endpoint and comparable June quarters. No group EBITDA/cash substituted.\\n- Horizon: five years to 19 October 2031. Equity hurdle: 15%, sensitivities 12%/18%. Analytical operating discount-rate range 12%–16%, central14%; not a claimed observed issuer WACC. Currency INR; financial tables ₹crore unless stated otherwise.\\n- Confidence: High on latest audited P&L, cash-flow classification and official historical tape; Medium on historical capital comparability, governance and physical production; Low on exact external-volume growth, unrestricted entry cash, current pledged status, binding financing, related LLP recovery and live market execution.\\n- March shareholding: promoter66.08%, parent Shera Energy51.01%; no March pledge shown. Current September pattern not located by the cutoff in the available primary index. Do not silently promote March disclosure into October clearance. Issued shares at quote date are independently matched to the official security master.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Decision consequence |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 integrity | Conditional pass | Unmodified audit/CARO and no reported fraud/default oppose a fail claim. Related-party concentration and LLP terms remain stewardship questions, not proven abuse. |\\n| G2 reliability | Conditional | Audited statements usable with identified regroupings; unreliable external-demand split leaves REV NS. Pending counterparty confirmations and historic reclassification remain explicit. |\\n| G3 funding | Hold / unresolved | No current committed-liquidity bridge. Modeled counterfactual breaches are not evidence that the issuer actually defaulted. No automatic fail-based IAS cap. |\\n| G4 tradability | Hold / restricted sizing | Measured L1 liquidity; live spread, current restrictions and dependable exit capacity unverified. |\\n\\nClose ₹61; issued shares22,215,000; market cap ₹135.5115cr. FY26 P/E 11.08x; TTM P/E 10.32x. March-anchored adjusted EV/EBITDA 8.87x, TTM 8.13x; not contemporaneous balance-sheet multiples. Mean/median60-session traded value ₹507317.50/₹320550.00. ₹5lakh exit at5% takes 31.196 sessions, versus 77.991 at2% and 15.598 at10%. One1500-share lot ₹91,500; five lots ₹457,500.\\n\\nCalendar-valid no-trade sessions are zero, not dropped. Last20 turnover is weaker than the full60-window; both are in the market audit. Market-maker status or a static spread parameter is not a live executable bid. At the 5% participation assumption, one lot already exceeds a typical day's conservative participation budget. A reported31-session exit estimate is an optimistic mechanical capacity calculation, not a promise of fill. Official daily ZIP URLs, content hashes and exact issuer-only rows are retained in `market_inputs.json`.\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\\n| Measure | Known sum | Coverage | Raw | Raw bound | Cap | Final/bound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 3.10 | 88.00% | NS | ['3.22', '4.30'] | None | NS; ['3.22', '4.30'] |\\n| GQS | 2.70 | 60.00% | NS | ['3.10', '6.70'] | 5 | NS; ['3.10', '5.00'] |\\n| IAS | 0.50 | 30.00% | NS | ['1.20', '7.50'] | None | NS; ['1.20', '7.50'] |\\n\\nAll NS entries are missing-data states, not zero or a neutral five. Bounds are mathematical possibilities, not statistical intervals or ranking scores. BQS known contribution is3.10 with12% missing; GQS known contribution2.70 with40% missing; IAS known contribution0.50 with70% missing, including the full25% BQS term. No weight redistribution or use of a BQS-bound midpoint occurs. The persistent CASH2/CAP3 cap limits GQS to5.0, including its upper bound. G1/G3 have not been asserted to fail; no unrelated governance/funding cap is manufactured. Calculations use Decimal with half-up presentation to one decimal, preserving exact raw numbers for classification.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence | Basis and contrary evidence |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | A10,A11 | Large related sales and unseasoned related LLP allocation constrain minority stewardship. Contrary: A01/A04 unmodified audits, outgoing-auditor no-issue statement and disclosed RPTs oppose integrity-fail inference. |\\n| MOAT | 3 | Medium | A15,A20 | Commodity conversion, price competition and concentrated group demand show weak barriers. Contrary: A13 physical scaling and process integration are useful but not demonstrated durable excess returns. |\\n| REV | NS | Low | A13,A14 | Nominal CAGR and aggregate output cannot establish like-for-like external physical demand throughFY26; no invented middle score. Contrary: A13 FY23–25 tonnes confirm real expansion; nominal results are not dismissed, but exact price/mix/scope bridge missing. |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | A14,A20 | Electrical metal/alloy end markets and expanded certified capacity give accessible room, tempered by customer concentration. Contrary: A15 uncontracted inventory and low switching costs make nameplate capacity insufficient evidence of demand. |\\n| CAP | 3 | Medium | A12,A18 | Committed-capital proxies include CWIP, rising ONWC and funding; observed 3-year incremental return~8.5% below12–16% analytical WACC range. Contrary: A14 new commissioning can have a lag; marginal economics may improve but no project cash IRR or order-linked returns disclosed. |\\n| CASH | 2 | High | A05,A09 | Two-year adjusted CFO−47.6527 versus positivePAT20.5011, before cash financing interest; repeated inventory absorption. Contrary: A05 reportedFY26CFO positive and revenue scaled, but supplier-finance growth explains the apparent conversion. |\\n| BAL | 3 | Medium | A06,A07 | Adjusted financing~111.96cr, short maturities and revocable rather than committed headroom limit resilience. Contrary: A16 ratings and no reported default support access, but group cash and facilities cannot prove issuer survival. |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | A14,A15 | Volume/capacity increased but cable/alloy project delayed and new capacity economics unseasoned. Contrary: A13 FY25 output rise is real evidence against a blanket poor-execution label. |\\n| EARN | 7 | Medium | A21,A18 | PAT and operating EBIT growth mostly operating, low other income; postIPO unchanged issued shares allow strongFY26 per-share progress. Contrary: A12 margin/utilization recovery on old assets is not proof of persistent new-project profit; FY25 dilution diluted earlier owners. |\\n| R | NS | Low | A06,A07 | No verified entry cash/funding bridge; uncommitted conditional bull is not a probability-weighted base shareholder return. Contrary: A21 operating profit momentum offers upside if funding and returns are independently established. |\\n| D | 2 | Medium | A09,A06 | Linked metal valuation/collection/refinancing stress exhausts bounded rescue; secured creditors precede ordinary equity. Contrary: A01 no current default reported, inventory sale proceeds can mitigate losses but recovery haircut unknown. |\\n| P | NS | Low | A07,A11 | Funded positive-skew setup cannot be established; no arbitrary probability weights or cheap-P/E substitute. Contrary: A14 capacity growth could create upside under independently verified cash economics. |\\n| L | 1 | High | A22 | INR5lakh requires31.196 sessions at5% conservative turnover, with eight zero sessions. Contrary: A22 shares traded52of60 sessions and a market maker exists; neither guarantees spread or exit liquidity. |\\n\\nWeights: BQS GOV20%, MOAT20%, REV12%, DUR10%, CAP12%, CASH10%, BAL8%, EXEC8%; GQS REV40%, DUR20%, EARN15%, CAP15%, CASH10%; IAS final-unrounded-BQS25%, R35%, D20%, P10%, L10%. `score_audit.json` stores every factor contribution and each bound. GOV±1 changes BQS known contribution±0.20; CAP±1 changes it±0.12 and GQS±0.15. The joint adverse/favourable movement does not remove missing REV, funding or execution restrictions, and does not create ranking eligibility. EARN concerns earnings sources/per-share growth; CASH alone carries the cash-conversion penalty. CAP addresses additional capital, BAL financing resilience, and GOV stewardship: the supplier-finance finding is not an unexplained discretionary deduction from all factors.\\n\\n## 4. Business Overview\\n\\nRajputana converts non-ferrous scrap and inputs into aluminium, copper, brass and other alloy products, including billets, rods, wire and newer cable/alloy lines. Industrial customers buy metal content plus conversion, processing and delivery, rather than a consumer brand franchise. The operation is centred in Rajasthan and linked operationally and commercially to the Shera group. Audited presentation is one manufacturing business; a reliably separated external cable/new-product revenue series was not located. Geography and new-product mix should not be inferred from group exports or a Zambia group-company advance.\\n\\nRevenue recognition is principally delivery/control transfer. Reported rupee growth reflects metal realizations, volume and mix. Input prices can pass through with a lag, while stock held ahead of orders exposes cash and valuation. Metal receipts, WIP conversion, customer collections, supplier settlement and financier settlement are distinct stages. A financier paying a supplier does not mean the issuer generated operating cash. Exact external-sale tonnes, yields, material recovery spreads and cash conversion by customer/product are the economic data needed to distinguish conversion profit from metal-price turnover. [CARE rationale](https://www.careratings.com/upload/CompanyFiles/PR/202601140142_Rajputana_Industries_Limited.pdf)\\n\\nFY26 related goods sales were₹335.5835cr, about48% of sales; related purchases₹257.6463cr. Related balances include receivables₹6.8746cr, trade payables₹24.6449cr and a Zambia-group advance₹1.8931cr. These relationships give demand access and supply coordination, but neither prove independent demand nor establish arm's-length profitability. IPO-era group-customer exposure declined from93.66%FY22 to90.60%FY23 and51.33%FY24; FY24 top-ten customers still represented73.54%, and top-ten suppliers79.49%. The increase in customer count from29 to120 is breadth, not proof of repeat profitable demand. [Prospectus](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/Prospectus.pdf)\\n\\n## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\\nThe positive case is integrated processing, accumulated operating know-how, capacity and group distribution. These could support consistent conversion quality and lower transaction cost. The counterevidence is stronger for now: fragmented metal conversion, commodity-linked prices, concentrated counterparties, thin operating spreads and weak incremental returns. CARE attributed FY25 margin compression partly to cash discounts used to grow volumes. Price pass-through is a protection against some metal risk, not an automatic moat or gross-margin guarantee.\\n\\nRelevant competitive mechanisms include scrap sourcing/recovery yield, energy cost, qualification, alloy consistency, delivery reliability and financing cost. Integrated recyclers such as [Gravita](https://www.gravitaindia.com/), copper-product processors such as [Bhagyanagar](https://bhagyanagarindia.com/), winding-wire/conductor specialists such as [Precision Wires](https://precisionwires.in/) and group-company Shera are mechanism comparators, not clean valuation peers. These links establish business overlap only, not dated cutoff valuation evidence. Different metals, integration, financial scope and product mix preclude borrowing their multiples without a dated normalized comparison. No unverified peer price, market share or franchise premium is used here. MOAT3 follows observed economics, not the mere absence of a famous brand.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\\n| Period | Start | End | CAGR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 3y | 254.67 | 698.47 | 39.98% |\\n| 4y | 244.35 | 698.47 | 30.03% |\\n| 5y | 185.22 | 698.47 | 30.40% |\\n\\nFY22–FY26 nominal revenue endpoints are244.3508,254.6650,326.5056,552.4081 and698.4671. Annual additions are10.3142,71.8406,225.9025 and146.0590cr; the largest addition was FY25, not a smooth annual compounding path. The five-year endpoint uses FY21 summary revenue185.2220; a full ten-year comparable series is not established and is not invented.\\n\\nAggregate production was4802.49MT FY22;5093.55 FY23;6593.88 FY24;10435.69 FY25. That is substantial genuine scaling through FY25. FY26 management's rounded84% utilization on13150MT implies11046MT, only about5.85% growth against26.44% sales growth. It is **derived production, not audited external-sale volume**. Product labels/realizations differ between the prospectus and later presentation; intermediate processing may cause double counting. This prevents attributing the residual mechanically to metal prices alone. Value added before conversion costs, calculated revenue minus materials and inventory change, is a useful cross-check but includes mix, accounting classification and stock effects and is not gross profit or pure volume.\\n\\nThe v3 starting score on >30% nominal CAGR would be9, limited by evidence/history rules. However, commodity-price pass-through and uncertain external-tonnage scope materially obscure the basis actually to score. **REV remains NS**, rather than choosing8, penalizing every other factor for the same issue, or calling the reported growth organic. No acquired business revenue is identified in the historical period; the absence of an acquisition does not by itself resolve metal-price inflation, product mix or intra-group demand. [December2025 presentation](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/RIL%20INVESTOR%20PRESENTATION%2031.12.2025.pdf)\\n\\nQ1FY27 revenue ₹204.0849cr, PAT ₹3.6902cr versus ₹167.1117cr/₹2.7861cr. TTM June2026 revenue ₹735.4403cr, operating EBITDA ₹30.4387cr and PAT ₹13.1361cr. Formula FY26+Q1FY27−Q1FY26; TTM cash flow ND.\\n\\nOne positive quarter is not a sustained trajectory upgrade or an annual cash-flow forecast. The model allocates actual Q1 P&L across its months only for an explicitly hypothetical cash bridge; subsequent activity pace is an independent assumption with700/800/900cr sensitivities.\\n\\n## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\\nPost-year certified capacity is18100MT: aluminium5800, copper4300, brass5600 and nickel/alloy2400, plus36000KM of cables. The revision is not backdated to FY26 and KM is never added to tonnes. Capacity provides an opportunity, not a contracted order book. Saleable yields, externally sold volumes and product-level utilization are still needed.\\n\\nThe model's base revenue path820/900/980/1050/1120cr corresponds to analytical metal-equivalent tonnes12500/13500/14500/15300/16000 and implied realizations. Bull860/1000/1150/1300/1450 corresponds13000/14500/16000/17500/18100. Bear620/620/650/680/700 corresponds10000/10000/10500/11000/11200. These are explicit assumptions, not audited physical forecasts; mix/realization growth does part of the work, particularly in the bull case. Separate cable revenue is not added on top of already assumed equivalent revenue. `calculation_rows.json` shows every implied realization and capacity boundary.\\n\\nFunding is not a footnote. Even growth at improved margins consumes inventory, receivables, supplier advances, restricted deposits and fixed assets. Base target ratios are receivables4.2%, inventory19.5%, other operating assets1.0%, ordinary payables7.8%, advances0.13% and fixed assets8.5% of sales. Supplier-finance debt is **not** subtracted from ONWC. Bull assumes inventory16%, receivables3.5%, operating payables8%, and margins rising4.5% to4.8%; this is better execution, not guaranteed cash release. Bear combines weaker margins with inventory24%, receivables6.5%, reduced supplier terms and explicit impairment replacement cash.\\n\\n| Window | ΔNOPAT | ΔIC | Incremental proxy | Net fixed investment | Δbroad ONWC | Residual |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 3y | 10.99 | 128.61 | 8.54% | 26.38 | 106.77 | -4.55 |\\n| 4y | 11.33 | 143.48 | 7.90% | 26.10 | 123.49 | -6.10 |\\n| 2y | 7.19 | 109.76 | 6.55% | 21.84 | 90.17 | -2.26 |\\n\\nThese observed incremental financing-IC proxies are below an analytical12–16% cost-of-capital range. They include committed CWIP and supplier financing and are not project IRRs. The broad operating bridge includes other current assets and ordinary payables; its residual to financing IC is separately retained rather than silently assuming all advances/deposits/tax provisions are operating. For FY26 a more selective operating IC is about189.1749cr versus184.7648cr financing proxy, a4.4101cr definition difference. No high-precision project-return claim follows from either. The three-year number is approximately8.5%, and two-year approximately6.6%; adding the12.6798cr implied supplier-finance reclassification to openingFY23 IC raises the three-year incremental-return proxy to about9.5%, still below12–16%. This sensitivity carries the same historical classification uncertainty as the CFO analysis;  the four-year comparison is supplementary, not mislabeled five years. A consistent five-year incremental-capital bridge is ND. Newly commissioned equipment can have a lag and existing-plant utilization can lift NOPAT, so improvement cannot all be credited to new capex. CAP3 and CASH2 support the GQS cap; attractive end-market language cannot override it.\\n\\n## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\\n| Dated promise / decision | Evidence of delivery | What remains |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY25 IPO net proceeds21.3051cr for WC14,solar4.5,general2.8051 | FY25 utilization statement reports full use | Use is not an investment return; reconcile resulting cash productivity |\\n| Cable/alloy project originally23.15cr, revised20cr;14.91cr spent March2025 | CARE January2026 noted roughly3month delay; FY26 PPE/CWIP reconcile | Product-specific commissioning, qualified sales and post-tax cash returns |\\n| Expanded capacity, disclosed after FY26 | Revised certification18100MT plus36000KM | Actual external tonnage, yields and sustained utilization; no backdating |\\n| September2,2026 related LLP investment8.20cr, completion expected2–3months | Formal board disclosure names14.21% participation and1.46+6.74 funding | Actual payment, redeemability, security priority, captive-power savings and minority protections |\\n\\nFY22 statutory audit records a predecessor resignation and consideration of concerns, but the original resignation communication is not located. FY22 internal-audit reports were not considered; FY23/24 say they were. FY23 group-CIC wording is explicitly management-supplied and not separately evaluated. These are dated assurance boundaries, not current fraud allegations.\\n\\nFor FY25, Keyur Shah completed the audit May28,2025 and resigned May29 citing professional preoccupation; its letter explicitly rejects an audit-evidence inability and identifies no other concerns. Mohata Baheti separately resigned as **internal** auditor May29 to pursue opportunities, then became statutory auditor June4. The internal-to-statutory role change and older history deserve attention, but the primary letters do not support claiming an adverse statutory-audit exit. FY26's auditor, CARO and IFC reports are unmodified; no outgoing issues are identified. [Exact resignation disclosure](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAJPUTANA_29052025233253_REG30_2905_merged__1_.pdf)\\n\\nFY26 remuneration includes Naseem0.54cr and Shivani0.48cr, with related-family employment payments separately disclosed. A2.87cr Shivani loan was raised and repaid during FY26, not an outstanding year-end balance. No quantified evidence establishes diversion. However, deploying8.2cr into a newly formed related LLP when adjusted operating cash is negative needs a clear minority-shareholder economic case. Equity1.46cr and security contribution6.74cr are not automatically equivalent liquidity claims. Zero savings/recovery is assumed in the main model; it does not assign a market value of zero to the business, only refuses unsupported cash credit.\\n\\n## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\\n| FY | Revenue | Operating EBITDA | EBIT | PAT | CFO reported | Net capex | Reported FCF proxy | Adjusted financing | IC proxy |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2022 | 244.35 | 11.87 | 9.61 | 2.64 | 8.82 | 0.97 | 7.84 | 25.38 | 41.28 |\\n| 2023 | 254.67 | 12.41 | 10.06 | 3.10 | -3.38 | 2.06 | -5.44 | 30.02 | 56.16 |\\n| 2024 | 326.51 | 17.65 | 15.14 | 5.13 | 17.55 | 7.05 | 10.50 | 44.32 | 75.01 |\\n| 2025 | 552.41 | 18.20 | 15.39 | 8.27 | -22.75 | 17.76 | -40.51 | 73.32 | 135.06 |\\n| 2026 | 698.47 | 27.90 | 24.75 | 12.23 | 8.97 | 10.05 | -1.08 | 111.96 | 184.76 |\\n\\n| FY | ROIC proxy | ROCE proxy | ROE | AR days | Inventory days | Payables days | EBIT cover | Issued-share EPS |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2022 | NS | NS | NS | NS | NS | NS | 1.54 | 1.87 |\\n| 2023 | 15.46% | 20.66% | 13.90% | 1.10 | 98.99 | 90.12 | 1.56 | 2.02 |\\n| 2024 | 17.27% | 23.08% | 17.08% | 8.08 | 95.80 | 76.87 | 1.72 | 3.34 |\\n| 2025 | 10.97% | 14.65% | 17.09% | 9.78 | 66.59 | 39.30 | 3.08 | 3.72 |\\n| 2026 | 11.58% | 15.48% | 17.40% | 11.77 | 68.80 | 27.95 | 2.80 | 5.51 |\\n\\nOperating EBIT=PBT+finance cost−other income; EBITDA=EBIT+D&A. FY26 operating EBITDA27.9039 differs from headline28.3859 including other income. FY24 prospectus headline EBITDA18.1578 similarly exceeds operating17.6505. PAT remains reported; a supplementary normalized-PAT proxy removes other income at the model tax rate, not a claim about its actual tax treatment. Issued-share EPS uses year-end shares for comparable ownership economics; it is **not reported weighted-average diluted EPS**. Reported FY25 EPS and the year-end denominator differ because capital was issued during the year. No unsupported fully diluted warrant/ESOP denominator is invented.\\n\\nGross profit is ND on a consistently comparable basis. Revenue less raw material and inventory change is **value added before payroll, processing, power and other conversion costs**, not gross margin. Historical FY25 materials were regrouped from542.6396 to541.4804cr with corresponding other-expense change1.1592cr; use latest comparatives without changing PAT. FY24 equity32.5750 from the later comparative differs by0.0030 from prospectus32.5720. FY22 prospectus PAT2.6377 differs from the later annual-summary2.2649; the original FY22 report itself discusses Ind AS transition, so the difference is unresolved rather than casually attributed to Indian-GAAP conversion. The final prospectus is retained for that row. The input reconciliation register preserves all discrepancies.\\n\\n### Cash, supplier financing and funding\\n\\nFY26 reported CFO8.9713cr less financing-like other-financial-liability increase27.5696 gives **adjusted CFO−18.5983cr**. FY25 reported−22.7517 less6.3027 gives−29.0544cr. Supplier-finance obligations rose21.2646 to48.8343cr (note48.8344, a0.0001 rounding difference). Financiers paid suppliers; issuer finance charges4.5213cr and short-dated maturity obligations remain. This is neither sustainable operating credit nor customer collections. Cash interest8.8484FY26 and4.9827FY25 is in financing cash flow, so it has not already reduced reported CFO. [FY26 cash flow and note19](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/RIL%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf)\\n\\nReported3-year CFO/PAT 0.1471; five-year 0.2936. Robust FY25–26 adjusted CFO ₹-47.6527cr/PAT ₹20.5011cr = -2.3244. Three-year adjusted ratio bounded -1.7585 to -1.2637 because FY24 supplier-finance opening reclassification differs across reports.\\n\\nFY26 after adjusted CFO, netcapex10.0466 and cash interest8.8484, cash after reinvestment/interest is−37.4933cr; FY25 equivalent−51.7998cr. This is not attributed to capex depressing CFO: inventory, receivables, advances and financing classification explain CFO; capex depresses investing cash and FCF. The FY26 pre-working-capital cash figure28.2503 is reduced by inventory34.4845, receivables11.9894 and other current assets7.5895, partly offset by ordinary payables9.9938 and the financing-like27.5696, plus other movements and tax3.2174.\\n\\nThe FY24 comparative changed classification between trade payables and supplier-finance liabilities. Its financing increase is bounded2.2821–14.9619, rather than inventing a new FY23 audited balance. The FY24 comparative also reports17.5445 cash generated,0.0053 tax paid and17.5498 CFO, a0.0106 sign/reconciliation difference; retain the source value and flag it. These issues limit historical precision but do not explain away the robust two-year cash deficit.\\n\\nFY26 netcapex is corroborated by fixed-asset/CWIP movement plus depreciation. The2.4235 asset-sale amount matches related asset sales0.3535+2.07. FY25 purchase33.2045 less sale-or-convert15.4418 gives17.7627 net and reconciles fixed assets; gross cash capex may contain a CWIP transfer and is not represented as unambiguously external cash spend. Maintenance spend is not disclosed; forecast0.4–0.6% sales and a D&A-linked assumption are analytical, with completion-capex0/4/10 sensitivities. They are not evidence that depreciation equals actual replacement cost.\\n\\nInventory138.4465 includes134.3369 WIP, about97%; metal valuation, yield and processing-stage duplication are important. Gross receivables28.6638 lessECL0.1433; over-six-month balances1.2957. A blank not-due column does not establish all short-aged debtors are delinquent. Some counterparty confirmations remain pending, without a quantified affected population. Quarterly lender-submission differences for ECL/inventory were disclosed and inventory matched at year-end; monitor reconciliation without inferring deliberate misstatement.\\n\\nBorrowings63.1248 plus supplier finance48.8343 produce adjusted financing111.9591cr. Cash/current accounts available by the conservative March anchor are only0.0822; reported cash equivalents0.3201 include deposits. Total deposits3.5690 are not assumed withdrawable. Contractual gross term principal28.8924 and CC34.3341 are modeled separately. The carrying term amount28.7905 is not the cash principal; financing costs are not forgiven. Source maturity gross63.2265 less costs0.1009=63.1256 versus face63.1248 leaves a0.0008 rounding difference. Undrawn15.6658 is terminable, not an irrevocable commitment; rated101.43 is neither incremental headroom nor unrestricted cash. CARE's withdrawal followed request and lender NOC, not recorded default. Acuite's July2026 group cash23.50cr cannot be lent to the issuer by assumption. [Acuite group rationale](https://connect.acuite.in/company-details/41607)\\n\\nReceivable days use average balances/total sales as proxy; inventory/payable days use average balances/(materials+inventory change), not undisclosed credit purchases. They are consistent descriptive proxies, not exact payment terms. ROIC/ROCE use average financing-IC proxy, ROE average equity; EBIT cover is EBIT/finance cost. Contract assets are not separately material in the cited statements; no fabricated balance is added. Short-term lease rent0.2565 has no recognized long-term ROU/lease liability in the reviewed notes. Guarantee exposure0.507cr is stressed explicitly.\\n\\n## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\\n### Methods and current expectations\\n\\nClose ₹61; issued shares22,215,000; market cap ₹135.5115cr. FY26 P/E 11.08x; TTM P/E 10.32x. March-anchored adjusted EV/EBITDA 8.87x, TTM 8.13x; not contemporaneous balance-sheet multiples. Mean/median60-session traded value ₹507317.50/₹320550.00. ₹5lakh exit at5% takes 31.196 sessions, versus 77.991 at2% and 15.598 at10%. One1500-share lot ₹91,500; five lots ₹457,500.\\n\\nHistorical earnings and book multiples are diagnostic, not independent proof of cheapness. Multiples match standalone operating EBITDA and adjusted financing including supplier finance. Price-to-book does not make metal WIP cash. No unrestricted-deposit deduction is used for current EV. March liabilities may differ materially by October, so no apparently precise current target is inferred from stale balances.\\n\\nThe principal model is reinvestment-linked FCFF with explicit financing and dilution, cross-checked against4/6/8x terminal operating EBITDA, not a peer-average rerating. FCFF=NOPAT+D&A−maintenance/growth capex−change in **operating** WC−restricted-deposit additions−explicit LLP cash−one-time impairment/replacement cash. Interest, term repayment, revolving debt and equity financing sit in the cash bridge rather than FCFF. Loss scenarios do not receive an unusable tax credit. Assets, financing and equity proceeds are not counted twice.\\n\\n### Pre-entry and intraperiod survival\\n\\nThe audited March31 anchor is followed by seven periods through October19, then60 monthly periods toOctober2031. Actual Q1 revenue/EBITDA/D&A informs the first three monthly allocations, not actual monthly CFO. Subsequent pre-entry activity,4cr completion cash and WC movement are assumptions. Original supplier financing10.9653/7.4650/9.3747/21.0294cr matures in30/60/90/150day buckets, hence **before entry**. Equal splits inside broad source buckets are a modeling convention, not lender contractual dates. A default bridge scales operating balances with activity; an explicitly different better-turnover bridge uses half that increase. It does not erase the failed default bridge or assert the actual company defaulted.\\n\\nWithin each period, front-half operating cash arrives, WC/capex/debt/interest/LLP obligations are paid, then the remaining operating cash arrives. A monthly closing surplus cannot hide an earlier modeled deficit. Front fraction0/0.5/1 is tested. First breach is permanent; no later optional draw, terminal price or retained-profit projection revives an ordinary return. Subsequent schedules on failed paths are diagnostic exposure trajectories only.\\n\\nOptional finance is bounded and **uncommitted**: bank headroom15.6658cr at11%; supplier finance outstanding48.8344cr, five-month refinancing tenor and cumulative gross-rollover cap700cr; ordinary CC renewals; post-entry equity only, priced48.80,3% issue cost, gross11.1508cr and maximum2.285m new shares, matching authorized capital24.5m. The minimum required whole-share count is rounded upward, checked against both cash and share ceilings; no fractional share or unlimited plug. Earlier issuance is forbidden because observed October issued shares are unchanged. Different issue prices change actual available shares/cash under the same ceilings. No future shareholders' capital is treated as free value for today's holders.\\n\\nLLP cash8.20cr is paid in model month2 after entry, with September/October/November/December/January timing tests. Zero terminal recovery or power savings is assumed without repayment/ownership economics. The6.74 security component is not simultaneously deducted in cash and added back as a risk-free terminal asset.\\n\\n| Case | First cash breach | New shares | Terminal required/available cash | Return |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Default bear no_new_financing | 2026-04-30 operating_working_capital_absorption | 0 | 0.24 / -137.03 | NS |\\n| Default bear bounded_optional | 2026-09-30 maintenance_and_growth_capex | 0 | 50.24 / -106.72 | NS |\\n| Improved-turnover bridge bear | 2027-01-19 operating_working_capital_absorption | 2285000 | 50.24 / -96.94 | NS |\\n| Default base no_new_financing | 2026-04-30 operating_working_capital_absorption | 0 | 0.00 / -76.18 | NS |\\n| Default base bounded_optional | 2026-09-30 term_debt_principal | 0 | 50.00 / -46.20 | NS |\\n| Improved-turnover bridge base | 2027-12-19 interest_on_opening_debt | 2285000 | 50.00 / -39.96 | NS |\\n| Default bull no_new_financing | 2026-04-30 operating_working_capital_absorption | 0 | 0.00 / -44.19 | NS |\\n| Default bull bounded_optional | 2026-09-30 term_debt_principal | 0 | 50.00 / -14.21 | NS |\\n| Improved-turnover bridge bull | No preterminal breach | 0 | 97.66 / 15.66 | NS |\\n\\n### Improved-turnover bull, terminal funding fails\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | NOPAT | Capex | ΔONWC | FCFF | Interest | Term principal | New bank | Equity gross | End cash | End debt | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 860.00 | 38.70 | 25.74 | 4.44 | -24.03 | 41.43 | 11.74 | 9.75 | 3.73 | 0.00 | 27.42 | 111.42 | 22215000 |\\n| 2 | 1000.00 | 47.00 | 31.43 | 16.90 | 17.08 | 2.17 | 10.93 | 7.54 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 13.88 | 103.88 | 22215000 |\\n| 3 | 1150.00 | 55.20 | 37.00 | 18.50 | 18.30 | 5.47 | 10.34 | 4.72 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 6.89 | 99.16 | 22215000 |\\n| 4 | 1300.00 | 62.40 | 41.83 | 19.25 | 18.30 | 10.22 | 10.02 | 1.50 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 8.11 | 97.66 | 22215000 |\\n| 5 | 1450.00 | 69.60 | 46.66 | 20.00 | 18.30 | 15.02 | 9.98 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 15.66 | 97.66 | 22215000 |\\n\\n### Minimum simultaneously feasible15% margin test\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | NOPAT | Capex | ΔONWC | FCFF | Interest | Term principal | New bank | Equity gross | End cash | End debt | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 860.00 | 56.97 | 39.41 | 4.44 | -24.03 | 55.10 | 11.57 | 9.75 | 2.00 | 0.00 | 39.50 | 109.69 | 22215000 |\\n| 2 | 1000.00 | 66.24 | 45.83 | 16.90 | 17.08 | 16.57 | 10.74 | 7.54 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 40.50 | 102.16 | 22215000 |\\n| 3 | 1150.00 | 76.18 | 52.70 | 18.50 | 18.30 | 21.16 | 10.15 | 4.72 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 49.34 | 97.43 | 22215000 |\\n| 4 | 1300.00 | 86.11 | 59.57 | 19.25 | 18.30 | 27.96 | 9.83 | 1.50 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 68.45 | 95.93 | 22215000 |\\n| 5 | 1450.00 | 96.05 | 66.45 | 20.00 | 18.30 | 34.81 | 9.79 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 95.93 | 95.93 | 22215000 |\\n\\nThese tables preserve annual financing, but monthly events remain controlling for survival. Equity gross is not free operating cash, and full finance rollover receipts/repayments are retained in the event ledger. The minimum-feasible margin case is a counterfactual threshold, not a new bull forecast.\\n\\n### Earned terminal value and terminal-year cash\\n\\nYear6 unlevered FCFF is next-year NOPAT less3% growth investment in ending ONWC, fixed capital and unavailable deposits. Terminal EV=FCFF/(14%−3%), never an unexplained multiple expansion. Forecast maintenance and D&A offset at steady state only as an explicit assumption. The terminal-year test pays **all ending financing at its outset**, then runs12 monthly operating/capex/WC/deposit events with no new bank loan, supplier rollover or equity. This is a conservative exit/deleveraging condition, not a claim all contractual debt falls due that day. Required terminal cash and any incremental operating reserve are stored. Ordinary return requires the entire pre-entry, five-year and terminal-year tests to pass.\\n\\nTerminal equity=operating EV+ending cash−existing debt−incremental retained operating buffer; debt settlement and the debt deduction are the same claim, counted once. Only cash above the settlement/reserve requirement is distributable. The minimum cash floor is also preserved. The operating reserve is fixed nominal in the main case; a separately recomputed growing-reserve sensitivity deducts its perpetual growth reinvestment from terminalFCFF. Terminal cash cannot be both added to equity and reused to survive a deficit. A separately displayed discounted operating-value diagnostic uses explicit FCFF plus terminal EV, entry cash and entry debt; it does **not** allocate future issuance value transfers and is not an investor's diluted-share NPV. The terminal diluted shareholder price uses actual modeled ending shares. Failed paths have no ordinary target or CAGR, even where an algebraic EV is positive.\\n\\n### Reverse expectations, sensitivities and entry discipline\\n\\n| Test | Target terminal price | Minimum flat margin bracket | Feasible high price | Binding |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 12pct_hurdle | 107.50 | 6.62% to 6.62% | 242.56 | Cash survival plus price; conditional bridge |\\n| 15pct_hurdle | 122.69 | 6.62% to 6.62% | 242.56 | Cash survival plus price; conditional bridge |\\n| 18pct_hurdle | 139.55 | 6.62% to 6.62% | 242.56 | Cash survival plus price; conditional bridge |\\n| fivefold | 305.00 | 7.58% to 7.58% | 305.00 | Cash survival plus price; conditional bridge |\\n| tenfold | 610.00 | 12.26% to 12.26% | 610.00 | Cash survival plus price; conditional bridge |\\n\\nThe reverse calculation changes a single flat five-year EBITDA margin on the bull sales/working-capital/capacity path and the explicitly favourable half-WC pre-entry bridge. Each trial re-runs every cash event, bank/equity bound, maturity and terminal test; it is not a terminal-earnings shortcut. The minimum feasible margin can be determined by cash survival rather than exact price equality. The low/high bracket, all trial requests, failure paths and residual prices are retained. Required margins well above the latest approximately4% operating EBITDA margin would require evidence of a different conversion spread/product mix, not merely more metal turnover.\\n\\nThe full3×3 margin/WACC grid recomputes linked cash paths rather than interpolating valuations. Sensitivity rows separately change pre-entry activity/working capital/capex, cash timing/floor, bank access, supplier refinancing budget, LLP timing, issue price, inventory, margin, discount rate and terminal growth. All resolve to one canonical path registry. For every **fully cash-feasible** path,12/15/18% maximum entry prices equal terminal diluted price/(1+hurdle)^5; otherwise all entry limits are NS. They are scenario-conditioned arithmetic, not buy ranges. There is no supported probability-weighted return, R score, positive-skew P score or unconditional entry range at this cutoff.\\n\\n## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\\nThe strongest counterargument to the hold is that supplier finance and bank revolvers are normal, functioning tools for a growing metal business, the group has access to lenders, profits are rising, and inventory is realizable metal rather than obsolete software. That could allow survival and a higher-value mix ramp. The report does not equate standard supplier finance with fraud or proof of default.\\n\\nThe corresponding permanent-loss path is linked, not a list of independent haircuts: weaker realizations/volume reduce conversion profit; inventory remains elevated; receivables stretch; suppliers/financiers shorten terms; cash-funded capex and the LLP outflow remain; constrained equity issuance fails to cover the deficit. Bear simultaneously assumes2.85cr receivable replacement loss,13.84cr inventory replacement loss and0.507cr guarantee call. It does not write down WC, subtract the same loss again in terminal value and claim three losses. Failure persists even if later annual profitability recovers. Shares rank behind secured debt and supplier-finance claims; a book-value floor is not supported.\\n\\nActual recovery value depends on inventory grade, lien priority, forced-sale discount, payable offsets and enforcement costs, none sufficiently verified. No arbitrary50% liquidation recovery or standard CAGR is assigned after insolvency. A conservative range includes severe or complete ordinary-equity impairment; an exact default probability is NS. Thesis-break variable: sustainable **cash after operating WC, replacement capex and interest**, combined with maturity cover, not reported PAT alone.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\\nAt ₹61, fivefold means₹305 and tenfold₹610 per original share after five years, before dividends. With no dilution, that is₹677.5575cr/₹1355.115cr equity; with the maximum24.5m shares it becomes₹747.25cr/₹1494.50cr. Dilution raises the required business equity value; a bonus/split would not count as economic growth.\\n\\nThe reverse table above solves the simultaneous funded path and price targets, keeping the same sales/capital-intensity constraints. It does not simply multiply FY26 PAT. An alternative route through much higher revenue requires extra metal/cable capacity, customer absorption and a funded WC/fixed-investment bridge; it is not free operating leverage. At current thin margins, low incremental returns and no verified current cash bridge, neither5× nor10× is a credible base case. Exceptional higher-margin solutions are analytical hurdle tests, not estimates that the company will achieve them. Capacity, returns, financing, per-share dilution and terminal liquidity all need to work together.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\\n| KPI / event | Baseline | Evidence required / warning |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Adjusted cash conversion | FY25–26 CFO−47.6527cr before financing interest | Next reporting period reconciles supplier finance; sustained positive cash after interest/capex, not only positive reported CFO |\\n| Entry/current free cash and headroom | March available0.0822; headroom15.6658 revocable | Dated bank evidence, lien status, covenant and written availability; no support if limits are withdrawn |\\n| Supplier financing | March48.8344cr, all original maturities≤150days | Current financier balances, paid maturities, fresh draw terms and gross turnover; overdue/accelerated demand is a thesis break |\\n| Inventory / external volumes | Inventory138.4465, WIP134.3369; FY26 exact external tonnesND | Product-wise tonnes, realizations, yield and NRV; persistent inventory growth faster than verified demand is a warning |\\n| Receivables / confirmations | Gross28.6638,ECL0.1433,>6months1.2957 | Confirmations and collection receipts; ageing/ECL deterioration without collections challenges earnings credibility |\\n| Capacity economics | Revised18100MT+36000KM | Actual output/sales by line, energy/conversion spread and post-tax cash return; no count of intermediate product twice |\\n| Related LLP | Approved8.20cr, expected2–3months fromSep2 | Payment dates, security redemption/priority, power contract and savings; unbounded additional group funding invalidates model limits |\\n| Additional capital return | Three-year proxy~8.5%, below12–16% range | Reconciled operating IC and commissioned cohorts exceeding capital cost, not just higher turnover |\\n| Governance/share capital | Promoter66.08%March; issued22.215mOctober | Latest pattern/encumbrances, new issuance terms, RPT pricing, auditor/confirmation resolution |\\n| Market execution | L1;5% reference exit31.196sessions | Verified current spread/restrictions and multiple-session depth; inability to execute even one lot blocks practical entry |\\n\\n## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\\n**Unranked, funding-and-execution hold. No compounder designation and no unconditional buy range.** The v3 quality/attractiveness matrix requires scoreability and gates before quadrant assignment. BQS/GQS/IAS are NS with disclosed bounds; assigning a quality/value quadrant from midpoint bounds would be false precision. This is not a low numeric IAS masquerading as a full assessment.\\n\\nA prospective purchase requires first resolving current liquidity and binding financing, verifying external-volume economics and new-capacity returns, and establishing executable liquidity. Only then does a price-dependent return spread become meaningful. Existing owners would need to monitor refinancing, related allocations and liquidity closely; this report does not issue an instruction to transact. A cheaper quote can improve a funded scenario's return but does not authorize treating an already breached cash path as investable.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\\n| ID | Document/date | Reviewed scope |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| E01 | [Annual Report FY2025–26](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/RIL%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf) 2026-09-04 | Printed73–133 audits, notes, cash flows; printed27–72 directors/secretarial/MD&A |\\n| E02 | [Annual Report FY2024–25](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/RIL%20Annual%20Report%202024-25.pdf) 2025-08-28 | Printed60–114 auditor/CARO/IFC and full statements/notes; printed26–59 governance |\\n| E03 | [Final IPO prospectus](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/Prospectus.pdf) 2024-08 | Printed31–45 risks,64–67 restated history,110–112 KPIs,141–160 business,210–273 restatement and notes |\\n| E04 | [December2025 investor presentation](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/RIL%20INVESTOR%20PRESENTATION%2031.12.2025.pdf) 2026-02-16 | Slide15 capacity and volume; management information |\\n| E05 | [June2026 NSE integrated filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ixbrl/INTEGRATED_FILING_INDAS_174728_15072026164133_iXBRL_WEB.html) 2026-07-15 | Standalone Q1FY27, limited-reviewed; no quarter cash flow |\\n| E07 | [Shareholding March2026](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/SHP%2031.03.2026.pdf) 2026-03-31 | Promoters/encumbrances; no outstanding convertible claims located |\\n| E06 | [CARE rating reaffirmation and withdrawal](https://www.careratings.com/upload/CompanyFiles/PR/202601140142_Rajputana_Industries_Limited.pdf) 2026-01-08 | All six pages, standalone with parent linkage; withdrawal bankNOC, not default |\\n| E08 | [Acuite rating upgrade](https://connect.acuite.in/company-details/41607) 2026-07-15 | Full rationale and facility annexure; GROUP consolidated, never substitute for issuer liquidity |\\n| E09 | [Related LLP investment approval](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAJPUTANA_03092026093524_Reg_30_02092026.pdf) 2026-09-03 | Both pages,1.46 equity+6.74 security contribution,14.21%,2–3 months |\\n| E10 | [Original audited standalone FY22](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/rajputana-standalone-financial-statements-fy-2021-2022.pdf) 2022-09-02 | Full signed auditor report, CARO and IFC; balance sheet/P&L. Historical canonical numbers use final prospectus and later comparable regroupings where identified. |\\n| E11 | [Original audited standalone FY23](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/Standalone%20Financial%20Statements%20Fy%202022-2023%20RIL.pdf) 2023-05-20 | Full signed auditor report, CARO and IFC; balance sheet/P&L. Historical canonical numbers use final prospectus and later comparable regroupings where identified. |\\n| E12 | [Original audited standalone FY24](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/Rajputana%20Standalone%20Financials%202023-24.pdf) 2024-05-06 | Full signed auditor report, CARO and IFC; balance sheet/P&L. Historical canonical numbers use final prospectus and later comparable regroupings where identified. |\\n| E13 | [June2025 NSE integrated filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ixbrl/INTEGRATED_FILING_INDAS_108728_06082025120032_iXBRL_WEB.html) 2025-08-06 | Standalone limited-reviewed quarter, comparison for TTM only. |\\n| E14 | [Statutory and internal auditor resignation disclosure and signed letters](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAJPUTANA_29052025233253_REG30_2905_merged__1_.pdf) 2025-05-29 | All six pages including signed scanned letters independently visually checked. Statutory professional preoccupation, no audit-evidence limitation or other concerns; separate internal auditor role change. |\\n\\nEvidence IDs A01–A22 map claims to precise primary sections in `evidence_ledger.json`; original source hashes are in `source_register.json`. Official market ZIP URLs and hashes are in the market input. This deliverable contains analytical paraphrases and issuer-only market extracts, not republished annual-report scans. Both compact and full bundles contain the minimal inputs, source ledger, narrative template and executable rebuild code; compact is not merely a reading copy. Full includes generated canonical paths/events and audits. Neither depends on private source caches or transfer helpers to recompute results.\\n\\nOpen questions, in decision order: (1) current unrestricted cash, debt and irrevocable liquidity commitments; (2) settlement/refinancing of March supplier maturities and current finance terms; (3) exact FY26 external sale tonnes and product price/mix bridge; (4) LLP payment, redemption and power-economics terms; (5) confirmation gaps and historical classification reconciliation; (6) separately measured newly commissioned capital returns; (7) current shareholding/pledges, warrants/ESOPs and execution restrictions. These missing items are not fabricated as zero, assumed benign, or hidden behind ratings.\\n\\nEvery figure is identifiable as source fact, calculation, analytical assumption or gap. The forecast is a stress/valuation framework, not management guidance. The registry contains **143 distinct cash-linked paths**, including failed sensitivities and reverse-search trials. `README.md` describes reconstruction, unit conventions, the no-post-breach-return rule and deterministic integrity checks. No probability weights, magical cash plugs, unlimited rescue, unsupported recovery asset, group-to-issuer cash transfer, or post-cutoff result is used.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive Verdict\"","\"\\n**Funding-and-execution hold, not a compounder.** This is the non-ferrous scrap-to-metal-products manufacturer Rajputana Industries Limited, NSE **RAJINDLTD**, ISIN **INE0PCU01012**, not Rajputana Biodiesel, Rajputana Stainless or Rajputana Investment & Finance. Physical output and operating profit have grown, and expanded capacity creates an identifiable opportunity. The decisive weakness is the capital and cash required to sustain that growth: FY26 reported operating cash flow includes a large increase in supplier financing. After reclassification, cash conversion is deeply negative, while old supplier-finance maturities expired before the entry date and current committed liquidity is unverified. At ₹61, a low-looking earnings multiple is not sufficient. This is a cyclical conversion/capacity thesis with refinancing dependence, not a proven high-return organic compounder. Auditable entry liquidity, supplier-finance continuity, related LLP terms and external-volume economics could change the conclusion; a lower price alone cannot fix a funding gap. [FY26 annual report](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/RIL%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf)\\n\\n\"","\"Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\"","\"\\n- Research information cutoff: **20 October 2026, Asia/Kolkata**. Valuation quote: official NSE close **19 October 2026**, not an intraday or subsequent price. Later retrieval does not make later information eligible.\\n- Scope: standalone Ind AS operating company. Shera group rating aggregates are not issuer accounts. Five audited financial years FY22–FY26, with FY21 nominal revenue endpoint and comparable June quarters. No group EBITDA/cash substituted.\\n- Horizon: five years to 19 October 2031. Equity hurdle: 15%, sensitivities 12%/18%. Analytical operating discount-rate range 12%–16%, central14%; not a claimed observed issuer WACC. Currency INR; financial tables ₹crore unless stated otherwise.\\n- Confidence: High on latest audited P&L, cash-flow classification and official historical tape; Medium on historical capital comparability, governance and physical production; Low on exact external-volume growth, unrestricted entry cash, current pledged status, binding financing, related LLP recovery and live market execution.\\n- March shareholding: promoter66.08%, parent Shera Energy51.01%; no March pledge shown. Current September pattern not located by the cutoff in the available primary index. Do not silently promote March disclosure into October clearance. Issued shares at quote date are independently matched to the official security master.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Decision consequence |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 integrity | Conditional pass | Unmodified audit/CARO and no reported fraud/default oppose a fail claim. Related-party concentration and LLP terms remain stewardship questions, not proven abuse. |\\n| G2 reliability | Conditional | Audited statements usable with identified regroupings; unreliable external-demand split leaves REV NS. Pending counterparty confirmations and historic reclassification remain explicit. |\\n| G3 funding | Hold / unresolved | No current committed-liquidity bridge. Modeled counterfactual breaches are not evidence that the issuer actually defaulted. No automatic fail-based IAS cap. |\\n| G4 tradability | Hold / restricted sizing | Measured L1 liquidity; live spread, current restrictions and dependable exit capacity unverified. |\\n\\nClose ₹61; issued shares22,215,000; market cap ₹135.5115cr. FY26 P/E 11.08x; TTM P/E 10.32x. March-anchored adjusted EV/EBITDA 8.87x, TTM 8.13x; not contemporaneous balance-sheet multiples. Mean/median60-session traded value ₹507317.50/₹320550.00. ₹5lakh exit at5% takes 31.196 sessions, versus 77.991 at2% and 15.598 at10%. One1500-share lot ₹91,500; five lots ₹457,500.\\n\\nCalendar-valid no-trade sessions are zero, not dropped. Last20 turnover is weaker than the full60-window; both are in the market audit. Market-maker status or a static spread parameter is not a live executable bid. At the 5% participation assumption, one lot already exceeds a typical day's conservative participation budget. A reported31-session exit estimate is an optimistic mechanical capacity calculation, not a promise of fill. Official daily ZIP URLs, content hashes and exact issuer-only rows are retained in `market_inputs.json`.\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and Calculation Audit\"","\"\\n| Measure | Known sum | Coverage | Raw | Raw bound | Cap | Final/bound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 3.10 | 88.00% | NS | ['3.22', '4.30'] | None | NS; ['3.22', '4.30'] |\\n| GQS | 2.70 | 60.00% | NS | ['3.10', '6.70'] | 5 | NS; ['3.10', '5.00'] |\\n| IAS | 0.50 | 30.00% | NS | ['1.20', '7.50'] | None | NS; ['1.20', '7.50'] |\\n\\nAll NS entries are missing-data states, not zero or a neutral five. Bounds are mathematical possibilities, not statistical intervals or ranking scores. BQS known contribution is3.10 with12% missing; GQS known contribution2.70 with40% missing; IAS known contribution0.50 with70% missing, including the full25% BQS term. No weight redistribution or use of a BQS-bound midpoint occurs. The persistent CASH2/CAP3 cap limits GQS to5.0, including its upper bound. G1/G3 have not been asserted to fail; no unrelated governance/funding cap is manufactured. Calculations use Decimal with half-up presentation to one decimal, preserving exact raw numbers for classification.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence | Basis and contrary evidence |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | A10,A11 | Large related sales and unseasoned related LLP allocation constrain minority stewardship. Contrary: A01/A04 unmodified audits, outgoing-auditor no-issue statement and disclosed RPTs oppose integrity-fail inference. |\\n| MOAT | 3 | Medium | A15,A20 | Commodity conversion, price competition and concentrated group demand show weak barriers. Contrary: A13 physical scaling and process integration are useful but not demonstrated durable excess returns. |\\n| REV | NS | Low | A13,A14 | Nominal CAGR and aggregate output cannot establish like-for-like external physical demand throughFY26; no invented middle score. Contrary: A13 FY23–25 tonnes confirm real expansion; nominal results are not dismissed, but exact price/mix/scope bridge missing. |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | A14,A20 | Electrical metal/alloy end markets and expanded certified capacity give accessible room, tempered by customer concentration. Contrary: A15 uncontracted inventory and low switching costs make nameplate capacity insufficient evidence of demand. |\\n| CAP | 3 | Medium | A12,A18 | Committed-capital proxies include CWIP, rising ONWC and funding; observed 3-year incremental return~8.5% below12–16% analytical WACC range. Contrary: A14 new commissioning can have a lag; marginal economics may improve but no project cash IRR or order-linked returns disclosed. |\\n| CASH | 2 | High | A05,A09 | Two-year adjusted CFO−47.6527 versus positivePAT20.5011, before cash financing interest; repeated inventory absorption. Contrary: A05 reportedFY26CFO positive and revenue scaled, but supplier-finance growth explains the apparent conversion. |\\n| BAL | 3 | Medium | A06,A07 | Adjusted financing~111.96cr, short maturities and revocable rather than committed headroom limit resilience. Contrary: A16 ratings and no reported default support access, but group cash and facilities cannot prove issuer survival. |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | A14,A15 | Volume/capacity increased but cable/alloy project delayed and new capacity economics unseasoned. Contrary: A13 FY25 output rise is real evidence against a blanket poor-execution label. |\\n| EARN | 7 | Medium | A21,A18 | PAT and operating EBIT growth mostly operating, low other income; postIPO unchanged issued shares allow strongFY26 per-share progress. Contrary: A12 margin/utilization recovery on old assets is not proof of persistent new-project profit; FY25 dilution diluted earlier owners. |\\n| R | NS | Low | A06,A07 | No verified entry cash/funding bridge; uncommitted conditional bull is not a probability-weighted base shareholder return. Contrary: A21 operating profit momentum offers upside if funding and returns are independently established. |\\n| D | 2 | Medium | A09,A06 | Linked metal valuation/collection/refinancing stress exhausts bounded rescue; secured creditors precede ordinary equity. Contrary: A01 no current default reported, inventory sale proceeds can mitigate losses but recovery haircut unknown. |\\n| P | NS | Low | A07,A11 | Funded positive-skew setup cannot be established; no arbitrary probability weights or cheap-P/E substitute. Contrary: A14 capacity growth could create upside under independently verified cash economics. |\\n| L | 1 | High | A22 | INR5lakh requires31.196 sessions at5% conservative turnover, with eight zero sessions. Contrary: A22 shares traded52of60 sessions and a market maker exists; neither guarantees spread or exit liquidity. |\\n\\nWeights: BQS GOV20%, MOAT20%, REV12%, DUR10%, CAP12%, CASH10%, BAL8%, EXEC8%; GQS REV40%, DUR20%, EARN15%, CAP15%, CASH10%; IAS final-unrounded-BQS25%, R35%, D20%, P10%, L10%. `score_audit.json` stores every factor contribution and each bound. GOV±1 changes BQS known contribution±0.20; CAP±1 changes it±0.12 and GQS±0.15. The joint adverse/favourable movement does not remove missing REV, funding or execution restrictions, and does not create ranking eligibility. EARN concerns earnings sources/per-share growth; CASH alone carries the cash-conversion penalty. CAP addresses additional capital, BAL financing resilience, and GOV stewardship: the supplier-finance finding is not an unexplained discretionary deduction from all factors.\\n\\n\"","\"Business Overview\"","\"\\nRajputana converts non-ferrous scrap and inputs into aluminium, copper, brass and other alloy products, including billets, rods, wire and newer cable/alloy lines. Industrial customers buy metal content plus conversion, processing and delivery, rather than a consumer brand franchise. The operation is centred in Rajasthan and linked operationally and commercially to the Shera group. Audited presentation is one manufacturing business; a reliably separated external cable/new-product revenue series was not located. Geography and new-product mix should not be inferred from group exports or a Zambia group-company advance.\\n\\nRevenue recognition is principally delivery/control transfer. Reported rupee growth reflects metal realizations, volume and mix. Input prices can pass through with a lag, while stock held ahead of orders exposes cash and valuation. Metal receipts, WIP conversion, customer collections, supplier settlement and financier settlement are distinct stages. A financier paying a supplier does not mean the issuer generated operating cash. Exact external-sale tonnes, yields, material recovery spreads and cash conversion by customer/product are the economic data needed to distinguish conversion profit from metal-price turnover. [CARE rationale](https://www.careratings.com/upload/CompanyFiles/PR/202601140142_Rajputana_Industries_Limited.pdf)\\n\\nFY26 related goods sales were₹335.5835cr, about48% of sales; related purchases₹257.6463cr. Related balances include receivables₹6.8746cr, trade payables₹24.6449cr and a Zambia-group advance₹1.8931cr. These relationships give demand access and supply coordination, but neither prove independent demand nor establish arm's-length profitability. IPO-era group-customer exposure declined from93.66%FY22 to90.60%FY23 and51.33%FY24; FY24 top-ten customers still represented73.54%, and top-ten suppliers79.49%. The increase in customer count from29 to120 is breadth, not proof of repeat profitable demand. [Prospectus](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/Prospectus.pdf)\\n\\n\"","\"Moat / Industry Position\"","\"\\nThe positive case is integrated processing, accumulated operating know-how, capacity and group distribution. These could support consistent conversion quality and lower transaction cost. The counterevidence is stronger for now: fragmented metal conversion, commodity-linked prices, concentrated counterparties, thin operating spreads and weak incremental returns. CARE attributed FY25 margin compression partly to cash discounts used to grow volumes. Price pass-through is a protection against some metal risk, not an automatic moat or gross-margin guarantee.\\n\\nRelevant competitive mechanisms include scrap sourcing/recovery yield, energy cost, qualification, alloy consistency, delivery reliability and financing cost. Integrated recyclers such as [Gravita](https://www.gravitaindia.com/), copper-product processors such as [Bhagyanagar](https://bhagyanagarindia.com/), winding-wire/conductor specialists such as [Precision Wires](https://precisionwires.in/) and group-company Shera are mechanism comparators, not clean valuation peers. These links establish business overlap only, not dated cutoff valuation evidence. Different metals, integration, financial scope and product mix preclude borrowing their multiples without a dated normalized comparison. No unverified peer price, market share or franchise premium is used here. MOAT3 follows observed economics, not the mere absence of a famous brand.\\n\\n\"","\"Revenue Growth & Scale\"","\"\\n| Period | Start | End | CAGR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 3y | 254.67 | 698.47 | 39.98% |\\n| 4y | 244.35 | 698.47 | 30.03% |\\n| 5y | 185.22 | 698.47 | 30.40% |\\n\\nFY22–FY26 nominal revenue endpoints are244.3508,254.6650,326.5056,552.4081 and698.4671. Annual additions are10.3142,71.8406,225.9025 and146.0590cr; the largest addition was FY25, not a smooth annual compounding path. The five-year endpoint uses FY21 summary revenue185.2220; a full ten-year comparable series is not established and is not invented.\\n\\nAggregate production was4802.49MT FY22;5093.55 FY23;6593.88 FY24;10435.69 FY25. That is substantial genuine scaling through FY25. FY26 management's rounded84% utilization on13150MT implies11046MT, only about5.85% growth against26.44% sales growth. It is **derived production, not audited external-sale volume**. Product labels/realizations differ between the prospectus and later presentation; intermediate processing may cause double counting. This prevents attributing the residual mechanically to metal prices alone. Value added before conversion costs, calculated revenue minus materials and inventory change, is a useful cross-check but includes mix, accounting classification and stock effects and is not gross profit or pure volume.\\n\\nThe v3 starting score on >30% nominal CAGR would be9, limited by evidence/history rules. However, commodity-price pass-through and uncertain external-tonnage scope materially obscure the basis actually to score. **REV remains NS**, rather than choosing8, penalizing every other factor for the same issue, or calling the reported growth organic. No acquired business revenue is identified in the historical period; the absence of an acquisition does not by itself resolve metal-price inflation, product mix or intra-group demand. [December2025 presentation](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/RIL%20INVESTOR%20PRESENTATION%2031.12.2025.pdf)\\n\\nQ1FY27 revenue ₹204.0849cr, PAT ₹3.6902cr versus ₹167.1117cr/₹2.7861cr. TTM June2026 revenue ₹735.4403cr, operating EBITDA ₹30.4387cr and PAT ₹13.1361cr. Formula FY26+Q1FY27−Q1FY26; TTM cash flow ND.\\n\\nOne positive quarter is not a sustained trajectory upgrade or an annual cash-flow forecast. The model allocates actual Q1 P&L across its months only for an explicitly hypothetical cash bridge; subsequent activity pace is an independent assumption with700/800/900cr sensitivities.\\n\\n\"","\"Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\"","\"\\nPost-year certified capacity is18100MT: aluminium5800, copper4300, brass5600 and nickel/alloy2400, plus36000KM of cables. The revision is not backdated to FY26 and KM is never added to tonnes. Capacity provides an opportunity, not a contracted order book. Saleable yields, externally sold volumes and product-level utilization are still needed.\\n\\nThe model's base revenue path820/900/980/1050/1120cr corresponds to analytical metal-equivalent tonnes12500/13500/14500/15300/16000 and implied realizations. Bull860/1000/1150/1300/1450 corresponds13000/14500/16000/17500/18100. Bear620/620/650/680/700 corresponds10000/10000/10500/11000/11200. These are explicit assumptions, not audited physical forecasts; mix/realization growth does part of the work, particularly in the bull case. Separate cable revenue is not added on top of already assumed equivalent revenue. `calculation_rows.json` shows every implied realization and capacity boundary.\\n\\nFunding is not a footnote. Even growth at improved margins consumes inventory, receivables, supplier advances, restricted deposits and fixed assets. Base target ratios are receivables4.2%, inventory19.5%, other operating assets1.0%, ordinary payables7.8%, advances0.13% and fixed assets8.5% of sales. Supplier-finance debt is **not** subtracted from ONWC. Bull assumes inventory16%, receivables3.5%, operating payables8%, and margins rising4.5% to4.8%; this is better execution, not guaranteed cash release. Bear combines weaker margins with inventory24%, receivables6.5%, reduced supplier terms and explicit impairment replacement cash.\\n\\n| Window | ΔNOPAT | ΔIC | Incremental proxy | Net fixed investment | Δbroad ONWC | Residual |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 3y | 10.99 | 128.61 | 8.54% | 26.38 | 106.77 | -4.55 |\\n| 4y | 11.33 | 143.48 | 7.90% | 26.10 | 123.49 | -6.10 |\\n| 2y | 7.19 | 109.76 | 6.55% | 21.84 | 90.17 | -2.26 |\\n\\nThese observed incremental financing-IC proxies are below an analytical12–16% cost-of-capital range. They include committed CWIP and supplier financing and are not project IRRs. The broad operating bridge includes other current assets and ordinary payables; its residual to financing IC is separately retained rather than silently assuming all advances/deposits/tax provisions are operating. For FY26 a more selective operating IC is about189.1749cr versus184.7648cr financing proxy, a4.4101cr definition difference. No high-precision project-return claim follows from either. The three-year number is approximately8.5%, and two-year approximately6.6%; adding the12.6798cr implied supplier-finance reclassification to openingFY23 IC raises the three-year incremental-return proxy to about9.5%, still below12–16%. This sensitivity carries the same historical classification uncertainty as the CFO analysis;  the four-year comparison is supplementary, not mislabeled five years. A consistent five-year incremental-capital bridge is ND. Newly commissioned equipment can have a lag and existing-plant utilization can lift NOPAT, so improvement cannot all be credited to new capex. CAP3 and CASH2 support the GQS cap; attractive end-market language cannot override it.\\n\\n\"","\"Management, Governance & Execution\"","\"\\n| Dated promise / decision | Evidence of delivery | What remains |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY25 IPO net proceeds21.3051cr for WC14,solar4.5,general2.8051 | FY25 utilization statement reports full use | Use is not an investment return; reconcile resulting cash productivity |\\n| Cable/alloy project originally23.15cr, revised20cr;14.91cr spent March2025 | CARE January2026 noted roughly3month delay; FY26 PPE/CWIP reconcile | Product-specific commissioning, qualified sales and post-tax cash returns |\\n| Expanded capacity, disclosed after FY26 | Revised certification18100MT plus36000KM | Actual external tonnage, yields and sustained utilization; no backdating |\\n| September2,2026 related LLP investment8.20cr, completion expected2–3months | Formal board disclosure names14.21% participation and1.46+6.74 funding | Actual payment, redeemability, security priority, captive-power savings and minority protections |\\n\\nFY22 statutory audit records a predecessor resignation and consideration of concerns, but the original resignation communication is not located. FY22 internal-audit reports were not considered; FY23/24 say they were. FY23 group-CIC wording is explicitly management-supplied and not separately evaluated. These are dated assurance boundaries, not current fraud allegations.\\n\\nFor FY25, Keyur Shah completed the audit May28,2025 and resigned May29 citing professional preoccupation; its letter explicitly rejects an audit-evidence inability and identifies no other concerns. Mohata Baheti separately resigned as **internal** auditor May29 to pursue opportunities, then became statutory auditor June4. The internal-to-statutory role change and older history deserve attention, but the primary letters do not support claiming an adverse statutory-audit exit. FY26's auditor, CARO and IFC reports are unmodified; no outgoing issues are identified. [Exact resignation disclosure](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAJPUTANA_29052025233253_REG30_2905_merged__1_.pdf)\\n\\nFY26 remuneration includes Naseem0.54cr and Shivani0.48cr, with related-family employment payments separately disclosed. A2.87cr Shivani loan was raised and repaid during FY26, not an outstanding year-end balance. No quantified evidence establishes diversion. However, deploying8.2cr into a newly formed related LLP when adjusted operating cash is negative needs a clear minority-shareholder economic case. Equity1.46cr and security contribution6.74cr are not automatically equivalent liquidity claims. Zero savings/recovery is assumed in the main model; it does not assign a market value of zero to the business, only refuses unsupported cash credit.\\n\\n\"","\"Financial Forensics\"","\"\\n| FY | Revenue | Operating EBITDA | EBIT | PAT | CFO reported | Net capex | Reported FCF proxy | Adjusted financing | IC proxy |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2022 | 244.35 | 11.87 | 9.61 | 2.64 | 8.82 | 0.97 | 7.84 | 25.38 | 41.28 |\\n| 2023 | 254.67 | 12.41 | 10.06 | 3.10 | -3.38 | 2.06 | -5.44 | 30.02 | 56.16 |\\n| 2024 | 326.51 | 17.65 | 15.14 | 5.13 | 17.55 | 7.05 | 10.50 | 44.32 | 75.01 |\\n| 2025 | 552.41 | 18.20 | 15.39 | 8.27 | -22.75 | 17.76 | -40.51 | 73.32 | 135.06 |\\n| 2026 | 698.47 | 27.90 | 24.75 | 12.23 | 8.97 | 10.05 | -1.08 | 111.96 | 184.76 |\\n\\n| FY | ROIC proxy | ROCE proxy | ROE | AR days | Inventory days | Payables days | EBIT cover | Issued-share EPS |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2022 | NS | NS | NS | NS | NS | NS | 1.54 | 1.87 |\\n| 2023 | 15.46% | 20.66% | 13.90% | 1.10 | 98.99 | 90.12 | 1.56 | 2.02 |\\n| 2024 | 17.27% | 23.08% | 17.08% | 8.08 | 95.80 | 76.87 | 1.72 | 3.34 |\\n| 2025 | 10.97% | 14.65% | 17.09% | 9.78 | 66.59 | 39.30 | 3.08 | 3.72 |\\n| 2026 | 11.58% | 15.48% | 17.40% | 11.77 | 68.80 | 27.95 | 2.80 | 5.51 |\\n\\nOperating EBIT=PBT+finance cost−other income; EBITDA=EBIT+D&A. FY26 operating EBITDA27.9039 differs from headline28.3859 including other income. FY24 prospectus headline EBITDA18.1578 similarly exceeds operating17.6505. PAT remains reported; a supplementary normalized-PAT proxy removes other income at the model tax rate, not a claim about its actual tax treatment. Issued-share EPS uses year-end shares for comparable ownership economics; it is **not reported weighted-average diluted EPS**. Reported FY25 EPS and the year-end denominator differ because capital was issued during the year. No unsupported fully diluted warrant/ESOP denominator is invented.\\n\\nGross profit is ND on a consistently comparable basis. Revenue less raw material and inventory change is **value added before payroll, processing, power and other conversion costs**, not gross margin. Historical FY25 materials were regrouped from542.6396 to541.4804cr with corresponding other-expense change1.1592cr; use latest comparatives without changing PAT. FY24 equity32.5750 from the later comparative differs by0.0030 from prospectus32.5720. FY22 prospectus PAT2.6377 differs from the later annual-summary2.2649; the original FY22 report itself discusses Ind AS transition, so the difference is unresolved rather than casually attributed to Indian-GAAP conversion. The final prospectus is retained for that row. The input reconciliation register preserves all discrepancies.\\n\\n### Cash, supplier financing and funding\\n\\nFY26 reported CFO8.9713cr less financing-like other-financial-liability increase27.5696 gives **adjusted CFO−18.5983cr**. FY25 reported−22.7517 less6.3027 gives−29.0544cr. Supplier-finance obligations rose21.2646 to48.8343cr (note48.8344, a0.0001 rounding difference). Financiers paid suppliers; issuer finance charges4.5213cr and short-dated maturity obligations remain. This is neither sustainable operating credit nor customer collections. Cash interest8.8484FY26 and4.9827FY25 is in financing cash flow, so it has not already reduced reported CFO. [FY26 cash flow and note19](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/RIL%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf)\\n\\nReported3-year CFO/PAT 0.1471; five-year 0.2936. Robust FY25–26 adjusted CFO ₹-47.6527cr/PAT ₹20.5011cr = -2.3244. Three-year adjusted ratio bounded -1.7585 to -1.2637 because FY24 supplier-finance opening reclassification differs across reports.\\n\\nFY26 after adjusted CFO, netcapex10.0466 and cash interest8.8484, cash after reinvestment/interest is−37.4933cr; FY25 equivalent−51.7998cr. This is not attributed to capex depressing CFO: inventory, receivables, advances and financing classification explain CFO; capex depresses investing cash and FCF. The FY26 pre-working-capital cash figure28.2503 is reduced by inventory34.4845, receivables11.9894 and other current assets7.5895, partly offset by ordinary payables9.9938 and the financing-like27.5696, plus other movements and tax3.2174.\\n\\nThe FY24 comparative changed classification between trade payables and supplier-finance liabilities. Its financing increase is bounded2.2821–14.9619, rather than inventing a new FY23 audited balance. The FY24 comparative also reports17.5445 cash generated,0.0053 tax paid and17.5498 CFO, a0.0106 sign/reconciliation difference; retain the source value and flag it. These issues limit historical precision but do not explain away the robust two-year cash deficit.\\n\\nFY26 netcapex is corroborated by fixed-asset/CWIP movement plus depreciation. The2.4235 asset-sale amount matches related asset sales0.3535+2.07. FY25 purchase33.2045 less sale-or-convert15.4418 gives17.7627 net and reconciles fixed assets; gross cash capex may contain a CWIP transfer and is not represented as unambiguously external cash spend. Maintenance spend is not disclosed; forecast0.4–0.6% sales and a D&A-linked assumption are analytical, with completion-capex0/4/10 sensitivities. They are not evidence that depreciation equals actual replacement cost.\\n\\nInventory138.4465 includes134.3369 WIP, about97%; metal valuation, yield and processing-stage duplication are important. Gross receivables28.6638 lessECL0.1433; over-six-month balances1.2957. A blank not-due column does not establish all short-aged debtors are delinquent. Some counterparty confirmations remain pending, without a quantified affected population. Quarterly lender-submission differences for ECL/inventory were disclosed and inventory matched at year-end; monitor reconciliation without inferring deliberate misstatement.\\n\\nBorrowings63.1248 plus supplier finance48.8343 produce adjusted financing111.9591cr. Cash/current accounts available by the conservative March anchor are only0.0822; reported cash equivalents0.3201 include deposits. Total deposits3.5690 are not assumed withdrawable. Contractual gross term principal28.8924 and CC34.3341 are modeled separately. The carrying term amount28.7905 is not the cash principal; financing costs are not forgiven. Source maturity gross63.2265 less costs0.1009=63.1256 versus face63.1248 leaves a0.0008 rounding difference. Undrawn15.6658 is terminable, not an irrevocable commitment; rated101.43 is neither incremental headroom nor unrestricted cash. CARE's withdrawal followed request and lender NOC, not recorded default. Acuite's July2026 group cash23.50cr cannot be lent to the issuer by assumption. [Acuite group rationale](https://connect.acuite.in/company-details/41607)\\n\\nReceivable days use average balances/total sales as proxy; inventory/payable days use average balances/(materials+inventory change), not undisclosed credit purchases. They are consistent descriptive proxies, not exact payment terms. ROIC/ROCE use average financing-IC proxy, ROE average equity; EBIT cover is EBIT/finance cost. Contract assets are not separately material in the cited statements; no fabricated balance is added. Short-term lease rent0.2565 has no recognized long-term ROU/lease liability in the reviewed notes. Guarantee exposure0.507cr is stressed explicitly.\\n\\n\"","\"Valuation & Market Expectations\"","\"\\n### Methods and current expectations\\n\\nClose ₹61; issued shares22,215,000; market cap ₹135.5115cr. FY26 P/E 11.08x; TTM P/E 10.32x. March-anchored adjusted EV/EBITDA 8.87x, TTM 8.13x; not contemporaneous balance-sheet multiples. Mean/median60-session traded value ₹507317.50/₹320550.00. ₹5lakh exit at5% takes 31.196 sessions, versus 77.991 at2% and 15.598 at10%. One1500-share lot ₹91,500; five lots ₹457,500.\\n\\nHistorical earnings and book multiples are diagnostic, not independent proof of cheapness. Multiples match standalone operating EBITDA and adjusted financing including supplier finance. Price-to-book does not make metal WIP cash. No unrestricted-deposit deduction is used for current EV. March liabilities may differ materially by October, so no apparently precise current target is inferred from stale balances.\\n\\nThe principal model is reinvestment-linked FCFF with explicit financing and dilution, cross-checked against4/6/8x terminal operating EBITDA, not a peer-average rerating. FCFF=NOPAT+D&A−maintenance/growth capex−change in **operating** WC−restricted-deposit additions−explicit LLP cash−one-time impairment/replacement cash. Interest, term repayment, revolving debt and equity financing sit in the cash bridge rather than FCFF. Loss scenarios do not receive an unusable tax credit. Assets, financing and equity proceeds are not counted twice.\\n\\n### Pre-entry and intraperiod survival\\n\\nThe audited March31 anchor is followed by seven periods through October19, then60 monthly periods toOctober2031. Actual Q1 revenue/EBITDA/D&A informs the first three monthly allocations, not actual monthly CFO. Subsequent pre-entry activity,4cr completion cash and WC movement are assumptions. Original supplier financing10.9653/7.4650/9.3747/21.0294cr matures in30/60/90/150day buckets, hence **before entry**. Equal splits inside broad source buckets are a modeling convention, not lender contractual dates. A default bridge scales operating balances with activity; an explicitly different better-turnover bridge uses half that increase. It does not erase the failed default bridge or assert the actual company defaulted.\\n\\nWithin each period, front-half operating cash arrives, WC/capex/debt/interest/LLP obligations are paid, then the remaining operating cash arrives. A monthly closing surplus cannot hide an earlier modeled deficit. Front fraction0/0.5/1 is tested. First breach is permanent; no later optional draw, terminal price or retained-profit projection revives an ordinary return. Subsequent schedules on failed paths are diagnostic exposure trajectories only.\\n\\nOptional finance is bounded and **uncommitted**: bank headroom15.6658cr at11%; supplier finance outstanding48.8344cr, five-month refinancing tenor and cumulative gross-rollover cap700cr; ordinary CC renewals; post-entry equity only, priced48.80,3% issue cost, gross11.1508cr and maximum2.285m new shares, matching authorized capital24.5m. The minimum required whole-share count is rounded upward, checked against both cash and share ceilings; no fractional share or unlimited plug. Earlier issuance is forbidden because observed October issued shares are unchanged. Different issue prices change actual available shares/cash under the same ceilings. No future shareholders' capital is treated as free value for today's holders.\\n\\nLLP cash8.20cr is paid in model month2 after entry, with September/October/November/December/January timing tests. Zero terminal recovery or power savings is assumed without repayment/ownership economics. The6.74 security component is not simultaneously deducted in cash and added back as a risk-free terminal asset.\\n\\n| Case | First cash breach | New shares | Terminal required/available cash | Return |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Default bear no_new_financing | 2026-04-30 operating_working_capital_absorption | 0 | 0.24 / -137.03 | NS |\\n| Default bear bounded_optional | 2026-09-30 maintenance_and_growth_capex | 0 | 50.24 / -106.72 | NS |\\n| Improved-turnover bridge bear | 2027-01-19 operating_working_capital_absorption | 2285000 | 50.24 / -96.94 | NS |\\n| Default base no_new_financing | 2026-04-30 operating_working_capital_absorption | 0 | 0.00 / -76.18 | NS |\\n| Default base bounded_optional | 2026-09-30 term_debt_principal | 0 | 50.00 / -46.20 | NS |\\n| Improved-turnover bridge base | 2027-12-19 interest_on_opening_debt | 2285000 | 50.00 / -39.96 | NS |\\n| Default bull no_new_financing | 2026-04-30 operating_working_capital_absorption | 0 | 0.00 / -44.19 | NS |\\n| Default bull bounded_optional | 2026-09-30 term_debt_principal | 0 | 50.00 / -14.21 | NS |\\n| Improved-turnover bridge bull | No preterminal breach | 0 | 97.66 / 15.66 | NS |\\n\\n### Improved-turnover bull, terminal funding fails\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | NOPAT | Capex | ΔONWC | FCFF | Interest | Term principal | New bank | Equity gross | End cash | End debt | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 860.00 | 38.70 | 25.74 | 4.44 | -24.03 | 41.43 | 11.74 | 9.75 | 3.73 | 0.00 | 27.42 | 111.42 | 22215000 |\\n| 2 | 1000.00 | 47.00 | 31.43 | 16.90 | 17.08 | 2.17 | 10.93 | 7.54 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 13.88 | 103.88 | 22215000 |\\n| 3 | 1150.00 | 55.20 | 37.00 | 18.50 | 18.30 | 5.47 | 10.34 | 4.72 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 6.89 | 99.16 | 22215000 |\\n| 4 | 1300.00 | 62.40 | 41.83 | 19.25 | 18.30 | 10.22 | 10.02 | 1.50 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 8.11 | 97.66 | 22215000 |\\n| 5 | 1450.00 | 69.60 | 46.66 | 20.00 | 18.30 | 15.02 | 9.98 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 15.66 | 97.66 | 22215000 |\\n\\n### Minimum simultaneously feasible15% margin test\\n| Year | Revenue | EBITDA | NOPAT | Capex | ΔONWC | FCFF | Interest | Term principal | New bank | Equity gross | End cash | End debt | Shares |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | 860.00 | 56.97 | 39.41 | 4.44 | -24.03 | 55.10 | 11.57 | 9.75 | 2.00 | 0.00 | 39.50 | 109.69 | 22215000 |\\n| 2 | 1000.00 | 66.24 | 45.83 | 16.90 | 17.08 | 16.57 | 10.74 | 7.54 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 40.50 | 102.16 | 22215000 |\\n| 3 | 1150.00 | 76.18 | 52.70 | 18.50 | 18.30 | 21.16 | 10.15 | 4.72 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 49.34 | 97.43 | 22215000 |\\n| 4 | 1300.00 | 86.11 | 59.57 | 19.25 | 18.30 | 27.96 | 9.83 | 1.50 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 68.45 | 95.93 | 22215000 |\\n| 5 | 1450.00 | 96.05 | 66.45 | 20.00 | 18.30 | 34.81 | 9.79 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 95.93 | 95.93 | 22215000 |\\n\\nThese tables preserve annual financing, but monthly events remain controlling for survival. Equity gross is not free operating cash, and full finance rollover receipts/repayments are retained in the event ledger. The minimum-feasible margin case is a counterfactual threshold, not a new bull forecast.\\n\\n### Earned terminal value and terminal-year cash\\n\\nYear6 unlevered FCFF is next-year NOPAT less3% growth investment in ending ONWC, fixed capital and unavailable deposits. Terminal EV=FCFF/(14%−3%), never an unexplained multiple expansion. Forecast maintenance and D&A offset at steady state only as an explicit assumption. The terminal-year test pays **all ending financing at its outset**, then runs12 monthly operating/capex/WC/deposit events with no new bank loan, supplier rollover or equity. This is a conservative exit/deleveraging condition, not a claim all contractual debt falls due that day. Required terminal cash and any incremental operating reserve are stored. Ordinary return requires the entire pre-entry, five-year and terminal-year tests to pass.\\n\\nTerminal equity=operating EV+ending cash−existing debt−incremental retained operating buffer; debt settlement and the debt deduction are the same claim, counted once. Only cash above the settlement/reserve requirement is distributable. The minimum cash floor is also preserved. The operating reserve is fixed nominal in the main case; a separately recomputed growing-reserve sensitivity deducts its perpetual growth reinvestment from terminalFCFF. Terminal cash cannot be both added to equity and reused to survive a deficit. A separately displayed discounted operating-value diagnostic uses explicit FCFF plus terminal EV, entry cash and entry debt; it does **not** allocate future issuance value transfers and is not an investor's diluted-share NPV. The terminal diluted shareholder price uses actual modeled ending shares. Failed paths have no ordinary target or CAGR, even where an algebraic EV is positive.\\n\\n### Reverse expectations, sensitivities and entry discipline\\n\\n| Test | Target terminal price | Minimum flat margin bracket | Feasible high price | Binding |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 12pct_hurdle | 107.50 | 6.62% to 6.62% | 242.56 | Cash survival plus price; conditional bridge |\\n| 15pct_hurdle | 122.69 | 6.62% to 6.62% | 242.56 | Cash survival plus price; conditional bridge |\\n| 18pct_hurdle | 139.55 | 6.62% to 6.62% | 242.56 | Cash survival plus price; conditional bridge |\\n| fivefold | 305.00 | 7.58% to 7.58% | 305.00 | Cash survival plus price; conditional bridge |\\n| tenfold | 610.00 | 12.26% to 12.26% | 610.00 | Cash survival plus price; conditional bridge |\\n\\nThe reverse calculation changes a single flat five-year EBITDA margin on the bull sales/working-capital/capacity path and the explicitly favourable half-WC pre-entry bridge. Each trial re-runs every cash event, bank/equity bound, maturity and terminal test; it is not a terminal-earnings shortcut. The minimum feasible margin can be determined by cash survival rather than exact price equality. The low/high bracket, all trial requests, failure paths and residual prices are retained. Required margins well above the latest approximately4% operating EBITDA margin would require evidence of a different conversion spread/product mix, not merely more metal turnover.\\n\\nThe full3×3 margin/WACC grid recomputes linked cash paths rather than interpolating valuations. Sensitivity rows separately change pre-entry activity/working capital/capex, cash timing/floor, bank access, supplier refinancing budget, LLP timing, issue price, inventory, margin, discount rate and terminal growth. All resolve to one canonical path registry. For every **fully cash-feasible** path,12/15/18% maximum entry prices equal terminal diluted price/(1+hurdle)^5; otherwise all entry limits are NS. They are scenario-conditioned arithmetic, not buy ranges. There is no supported probability-weighted return, R score, positive-skew P score or unconditional entry range at this cutoff.\\n\\n\"","\"Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\"","\"\\nThe strongest counterargument to the hold is that supplier finance and bank revolvers are normal, functioning tools for a growing metal business, the group has access to lenders, profits are rising, and inventory is realizable metal rather than obsolete software. That could allow survival and a higher-value mix ramp. The report does not equate standard supplier finance with fraud or proof of default.\\n\\nThe corresponding permanent-loss path is linked, not a list of independent haircuts: weaker realizations/volume reduce conversion profit; inventory remains elevated; receivables stretch; suppliers/financiers shorten terms; cash-funded capex and the LLP outflow remain; constrained equity issuance fails to cover the deficit. Bear simultaneously assumes2.85cr receivable replacement loss,13.84cr inventory replacement loss and0.507cr guarantee call. It does not write down WC, subtract the same loss again in terminal value and claim three losses. Failure persists even if later annual profitability recovers. Shares rank behind secured debt and supplier-finance claims; a book-value floor is not supported.\\n\\nActual recovery value depends on inventory grade, lien priority, forced-sale discount, payable offsets and enforcement costs, none sufficiently verified. No arbitrary50% liquidation recovery or standard CAGR is assigned after insolvency. A conservative range includes severe or complete ordinary-equity impairment; an exact default probability is NS. Thesis-break variable: sustainable **cash after operating WC, replacement capex and interest**, combined with maturity cover, not reported PAT alone.\\n\\n\"","\"5× / 10× Feasibility\"","\"\\nAt ₹61, fivefold means₹305 and tenfold₹610 per original share after five years, before dividends. With no dilution, that is₹677.5575cr/₹1355.115cr equity; with the maximum24.5m shares it becomes₹747.25cr/₹1494.50cr. Dilution raises the required business equity value; a bonus/split would not count as economic growth.\\n\\nThe reverse table above solves the simultaneous funded path and price targets, keeping the same sales/capital-intensity constraints. It does not simply multiply FY26 PAT. An alternative route through much higher revenue requires extra metal/cable capacity, customer absorption and a funded WC/fixed-investment bridge; it is not free operating leverage. At current thin margins, low incremental returns and no verified current cash bridge, neither5× nor10× is a credible base case. Exceptional higher-margin solutions are analytical hurdle tests, not estimates that the company will achieve them. Capacity, returns, financing, per-share dilution and terminal liquidity all need to work together.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring Plan\"","\"\\n| KPI / event | Baseline | Evidence required / warning |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Adjusted cash conversion | FY25–26 CFO−47.6527cr before financing interest | Next reporting period reconciles supplier finance; sustained positive cash after interest/capex, not only positive reported CFO |\\n| Entry/current free cash and headroom | March available0.0822; headroom15.6658 revocable | Dated bank evidence, lien status, covenant and written availability; no support if limits are withdrawn |\\n| Supplier financing | March48.8344cr, all original maturities≤150days | Current financier balances, paid maturities, fresh draw terms and gross turnover; overdue/accelerated demand is a thesis break |\\n| Inventory / external volumes | Inventory138.4465, WIP134.3369; FY26 exact external tonnesND | Product-wise tonnes, realizations, yield and NRV; persistent inventory growth faster than verified demand is a warning |\\n| Receivables / confirmations | Gross28.6638,ECL0.1433,>6months1.2957 | Confirmations and collection receipts; ageing/ECL deterioration without collections challenges earnings credibility |\\n| Capacity economics | Revised18100MT+36000KM | Actual output/sales by line, energy/conversion spread and post-tax cash return; no count of intermediate product twice |\\n| Related LLP | Approved8.20cr, expected2–3months fromSep2 | Payment dates, security redemption/priority, power contract and savings; unbounded additional group funding invalidates model limits |\\n| Additional capital return | Three-year proxy~8.5%, below12–16% range | Reconciled operating IC and commissioned cohorts exceeding capital cost, not just higher turnover |\\n| Governance/share capital | Promoter66.08%March; issued22.215mOctober | Latest pattern/encumbrances, new issuance terms, RPT pricing, auditor/confirmation resolution |\\n| Market execution | L1;5% reference exit31.196sessions | Verified current spread/restrictions and multiple-session depth; inability to execute even one lot blocks practical entry |\\n\\n\"","\"Final Classification & Conditional Action\"","\"\\n**Unranked, funding-and-execution hold. No compounder designation and no unconditional buy range.** The v3 quality/attractiveness matrix requires scoreability and gates before quadrant assignment. BQS/GQS/IAS are NS with disclosed bounds; assigning a quality/value quadrant from midpoint bounds would be false precision. This is not a low numeric IAS masquerading as a full assessment.\\n\\nA prospective purchase requires first resolving current liquidity and binding financing, verifying external-volume economics and new-capacity returns, and establishing executable liquidity. Only then does a price-dependent return spread become meaningful. Existing owners would need to monitor refinancing, related allocations and liquidity closely; this report does not issue an instruction to transact. A cheaper quote can improve a funded scenario's return but does not authorize treating an already breached cash path as investable.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\"","\"\\n| ID | Document/date | Reviewed scope |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| E01 | [Annual Report FY2025–26](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/RIL%20Annual%20Report%202025-26.pdf) 2026-09-04 | Printed73–133 audits, notes, cash flows; printed27–72 directors/secretarial/MD&A |\\n| E02 | [Annual Report FY2024–25](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/RIL%20Annual%20Report%202024-25.pdf) 2025-08-28 | Printed60–114 auditor/CARO/IFC and full statements/notes; printed26–59 governance |\\n| E03 | [Final IPO prospectus](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/Prospectus.pdf) 2024-08 | Printed31–45 risks,64–67 restated history,110–112 KPIs,141–160 business,210–273 restatement and notes |\\n| E04 | [December2025 investor presentation](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/RIL%20INVESTOR%20PRESENTATION%2031.12.2025.pdf) 2026-02-16 | Slide15 capacity and volume; management information |\\n| E05 | [June2026 NSE integrated filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ixbrl/INTEGRATED_FILING_INDAS_174728_15072026164133_iXBRL_WEB.html) 2026-07-15 | Standalone Q1FY27, limited-reviewed; no quarter cash flow |\\n| E07 | [Shareholding March2026](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/SHP%2031.03.2026.pdf) 2026-03-31 | Promoters/encumbrances; no outstanding convertible claims located |\\n| E06 | [CARE rating reaffirmation and withdrawal](https://www.careratings.com/upload/CompanyFiles/PR/202601140142_Rajputana_Industries_Limited.pdf) 2026-01-08 | All six pages, standalone with parent linkage; withdrawal bankNOC, not default |\\n| E08 | [Acuite rating upgrade](https://connect.acuite.in/company-details/41607) 2026-07-15 | Full rationale and facility annexure; GROUP consolidated, never substitute for issuer liquidity |\\n| E09 | [Related LLP investment approval](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAJPUTANA_03092026093524_Reg_30_02092026.pdf) 2026-09-03 | Both pages,1.46 equity+6.74 security contribution,14.21%,2–3 months |\\n| E10 | [Original audited standalone FY22](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/rajputana-standalone-financial-statements-fy-2021-2022.pdf) 2022-09-02 | Full signed auditor report, CARO and IFC; balance sheet/P&L. Historical canonical numbers use final prospectus and later comparable regroupings where identified. |\\n| E11 | [Original audited standalone FY23](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/Standalone%20Financial%20Statements%20Fy%202022-2023%20RIL.pdf) 2023-05-20 | Full signed auditor report, CARO and IFC; balance sheet/P&L. Historical canonical numbers use final prospectus and later comparable regroupings where identified. |\\n| E12 | [Original audited standalone FY24](https://rajputanaindustries.com/images/Rajputana%20Standalone%20Financials%202023-24.pdf) 2024-05-06 | Full signed auditor report, CARO and IFC; balance sheet/P&L. Historical canonical numbers use final prospectus and later comparable regroupings where identified. |\\n| E13 | [June2025 NSE integrated filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ixbrl/INTEGRATED_FILING_INDAS_108728_06082025120032_iXBRL_WEB.html) 2025-08-06 | Standalone limited-reviewed quarter, comparison for TTM only. |\\n| E14 | [Statutory and internal auditor resignation disclosure and signed letters](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAJPUTANA_29052025233253_REG30_2905_merged__1_.pdf) 2025-05-29 | All six pages including signed scanned letters independently visually checked. Statutory professional preoccupation, no audit-evidence limitation or other concerns; separate internal auditor role change. |\\n\\nEvidence IDs A01–A22 map claims to precise primary sections in `evidence_ledger.json`; original source hashes are in `source_register.json`. Official market ZIP URLs and hashes are in the market input. This deliverable contains analytical paraphrases and issuer-only market extracts, not republished annual-report scans. Both compact and full bundles contain the minimal inputs, source ledger, narrative template and executable rebuild code; compact is not merely a reading copy. Full includes generated canonical paths/events and audits. Neither depends on private source caches or transfer helpers to recompute results.\\n\\nOpen questions, in decision order: (1) current unrestricted cash, debt and irrevocable liquidity commitments; (2) settlement/refinancing of March supplier maturities and current finance terms; (3) exact FY26 external sale tonnes and product price/mix bridge; (4) LLP payment, redemption and power-economics terms; (5) confirmation gaps and historical classification reconciliation; (6) separately measured newly commissioned capital returns; (7) current shareholding/pledges, warrants/ESOPs and execution restrictions. These missing items are not fabricated as zero, assumed benign, or hidden behind ratings.\\n\\nEvery figure is identifiable as source fact, calculation, analytical assumption or gap. The forecast is a stress/valuation framework, not management guidance. The registry contains **143 distinct cash-linked paths**, including failed sensitivities and reverse-search trials. `README.md` describes reconstruction, unit conventions, the no-post-breach-return rule and deterministic integrity checks. No probability weights, magical cash plugs, unlimited rescue, unsupported recovery asset, group-to-issuer cash transfer, or post-cutoff result is used.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"original_heading\"","1285","\"## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\"","1334","4430","\"## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\"","4468","9742","\"## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\"","9766","11799","\"## 4. Business Overview\\n\"","11830","13237","\"## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\"","13266","15646","\"## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\"","15689","18845","\"## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\"","18886","21521","\"## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\"","21547","28645","\"## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\"","28684","39049","\"## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\"","39088","40704","\"## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\"","40732","41748","\"## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\"","41771","43755","\"## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\"","43804","44730","\"## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\"","44775","50059","\"## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\"","\"format\"","\"content_sha256\"","\"Complete JSON content\"","\"8ef08e266684552f8fc2c5d10a2ba365c24323cb973b2da6797cc86644463895\"","\"Authored analytical table or source register, not 